<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=plate-armour">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Weapons and war</p><h1 class="enc-title">Plate armour</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1st century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as panoply</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Plate armour is body armour made from bronze, iron or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> plates, which at its fullest enclosed all or most of the wearer. Partial bronze plate protecting the chest and limbs was used by the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> as early as the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>, and the Roman lorica segmentata was in use between the 1st century BCE and the 4th century CE. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> iron plate cuirasses were made in the Kofun period. Complete suits of steel plate had been developed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> by about 1420, and reached their peak in the 15th and 16th centuries before declining after the early 17th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/plate-armour.jpg" alt="Plate armour" width="330" height="428" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lossy-page1-2122px-Hästrustning_-_Livrustkammaren_-_72697_aaa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The city’s armourers were famous across Europe, supplying helmets and <b>plate armour</b> to princes and mercenary captains, while wool, fustian and above all silk, promoted by the Sforza, employed thousands of workers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Duchy of Milan · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1st century BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Roman Empire</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Also arose independently</span>Japan, Kofun period (250-538 CE)</p><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Partial bronze plate was used by the ancient Greeks as early as the late Bronze Age (the Dendra panoply) but no date is given; full steel plate suits are a European development of about 1420.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/bronze">Bronze<span>c. 4650 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/iron-smelting">Iron smelting<span>c. 2500 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=plate-armour">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
